## Completion

- Keep going when the next step does not need the user's input. Ask before destructive or hard-to-undo actions, or when you cannot continue without a decision.
- End task responses with one compact `Next:` line. List required actions before optional follow-up; write `Next: none` when complete.
- State `Status: Blocked` only when blocked; omit status otherwise. Claim completion only after verifying every explicit requirement; cite evidence and give exact commands or paths for user actions.

## Repository context

- Before working in a repository, read its root `AGENTS.md`, `PRODUCT.md`, and `GLOSSARY.md` when present, and follow applicable nested `AGENTS.md` files. Use the glossary's ubiquitous language consistently.

## Writing and documentation

- Apply ASD-STE100 to documentation when appropriate.
- Docs: when editing markdown for agents or contributors, keep runnable shell commands and concrete repo paths first; omit redundant meta commentary.
- Markdown: format Markdown files with `oxfmt --write <path-to-file.md>`.
- Format prose with Markdown, not ANSI terminal escape sequences. Before saving prose through MCP tools, verify it contains no unintended control characters.
- Human-facing prose: for new files meant for humans to read, load `writing-clearly-and-concisely` first and `humanizer` last; do not use em dashes.

## Git and GitHub

- Git commits: use Conventional Commits only (`fix`, `feat`, `build`, `chore`, `ci`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `revert`). Keep headers under 72 chars, lowercase subject, no period, prefer header-only.
- PR and issue images: on macOS, `GH_SESSION_TOKEN=$(mise run gh image extract-token) gh image --repo <owner/repo> <file>...` uploads each file to GitHub and prints its markdown; never print or log the token. Without a token, commit the files to an orphan branch `<issue>-screenshots`, link them as `https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/blob/<branch>/<file>?raw=true`, and delete the branch after the merge. Check that an image shows no customer data first.

## Browser, web, and cloud tools

- X/Twitter: use `bird` instead of browser automation.
- Browser use: load `agent-browser` first and use a task-scoped session as its skill directs. On macOS, connect that session to Helium for Agents with `agent-browser connect 9222`; if Helium is unavailable or the connection launches a fresh profile, close the session, run `mise run helium:run`, and reconnect. On headless Linux, use `agent-browser`'s managed headless browser directly and diagnose launch failures with `agent-browser doctor`. For Electron, follow the project `AGENTS.md` and its project-specific CDP port.
- Authenticated browsing: load `authenticating-browsers` before using saved website logins, refreshing login seeds on macOS, or diagnosing Chrome's sandbox on the headless Linux VPS (hostname `box`). Follow the documented per-site workflow; keep login seeds and runtime credentials out of logs and review artifacts.
{{ if eq .chezmoi.os "darwin" -}}
- Native desktop apps and OS dialogs: use `agent-desktop`. For Electron app content, follow the project's `agent-browser` workflow above. Before using `agent-desktop`, run `agent-desktop skills get desktop` and `agent-desktop skills get platform`.
- Android UI checks: drive the phone with `agent-device`, pinned to a version in unattended runs. The first snapshot installs a helper APK that may need one confirmation tap. Connect over wireless ADB (`adb devices -l`, then `adb mdns services`); if discovery fails, ask for the IP and port from the phone's Wireless debugging screen. If ADB still cannot connect, ask the user to start the ADB server from Ghostty, which works on this Mac.
{{ end -}}
- Tool and library docs: Use Context7 to find relevant documentation. Check the official docs or source when coverage is missing, results conflict, or exact version or current behavior matters. Prefer docs for the version in use.

## Remote and headless work

- Before desktop-dependent tasks, identify the execution host and available capabilities. `box` is headless Linux; the viewing device may be elsewhere, even when executing on macOS.
- On `box` (check `hostname`), end any reply that produced an artifact under `$HOME/projects` (HTML, report, screenshot) with its link: `https://box.javanese-pound.ts.net:8443/` plus the URL-encoded path relative to `$HOME/projects`. Tailnet-only; viewers need Tailscale. For files outside that tree, or on any other host, use an attachment. For public sharing, load `sharing-public-files`.
- For other remote handoffs, deliver reachable URLs or attachments, not executor-local paths or `localhost`. If unavailable, give exact SSH forwarding or file-transfer commands for the viewing device.
- For watchers, dev servers, and background shells, use task-owned Herdr panes when inside Herdr (load its skill); otherwise use harness-supported long-running processes. Match the pane's shell. Report temporary previews' URLs, access requirements, lifetime, and stop commands.
- Verify artifact URLs, assets, and interactions using the browser workflow above. Distinguish server checks from confirmed device access; request a device check only when troubleshooting access.
- Use documented device-code or remote OAuth flows; loopback callbacks target the execution host. Keep credentials out of artifacts and URLs. Replace desktop-only `open` commands, clipboard writes, and notifications with conversation content or links. Finish headless checks, then hand off native-app, simulator, desktop-session, or hardware checks to a capable machine.

## Shell and code quality

- Shell scripts: after modifying one, run `shfmt -w -ln <bash|posix|mksh|bats|zsh> <path-to-script>` with its dialect. Run `shellcheck <path-to-script>` for Bash or POSIX-compatible scripts, `bash -n <path-to-script>` for Bash, and `zsh -n <path-to-script>` for Zsh. Prefer `#!/usr/bin/env <interpreter>` over hardcoded paths like `#!/bin/bash`.
- Code cleanup: after a fix changes direction, audit for speculative or now-dead code from earlier attempts. Remove unused helpers/imports/types before final checks, then rerun the relevant typecheck/lint/format commands.
- Code comments: say what the code does and why it is that way now. Leave out tombstones (`# foo removed`) and incident history; git blame and commit messages carry that.
- UI: Keep copy task-focused. Use clear guidance for empty states and errors; avoid decorative narrative.
- UI options: when a design choice needs the user's decision, show a mockup. Follow the repo's design convention (for example Penpot in haul); without one, make a single self-contained HTML file. Load `html-prototype` and ground it in the repo's own design tokens. Use `html-wireframe` only for layout and structure questions.
- Design changes: when a design change is final, update the repo's design source (for example Penpot screens) in the same PR. When its tool is unreachable (for example the Penpot tab is closed), ask the user to open it.

## Worktrees

- When running in Herdr, prefer `herdr worktree` for a separate write-owning task that needs its own checkout; let native subagents share their parent checkout. Create the worktree beside the source checkout as `<repo-name>-<branch-name>`, with `/` in the branch replaced by `-`. Pass the absolute path to `--path` and the branch to `--branch`. If the destination exists, stop and report the collision.
- When a task wraps up (its PR is merged, or the user closes it), stop the agent sessions you started there, run `herdr worktree remove --workspace <id>` without `--force`, and delete the merged local branch. If the worktree is dirty or holds commits the merged PR lacks, stop and report instead.
- Keep one writing agent per worktree. To move a task to another agent, stop the first session and start the next one in the same worktree.
- Do not move or edit harness session files to imitate a migration.
- When a task spans several checkouts, pass the target to every git and gh command (`git -C <path>`, `gh --repo <owner/name>`).
